= BITwiki Constitution =

== Preamble ==

Knowledge is worth pursuing for its own sake. We learn out of curiosity, necessity, wonder, and the desire to understand the world we inhabit. No authority can cultivate wisdom on another's behalf. Understanding asks something of us: time, attention, criticism, practice, and the willingness to discover that we were mistaken.

We do not begin from nothing. We inherit languages, tools, questions, discoveries, and mistakes from minds that came before us. To think independently is not to think alone. Intellectual sovereignty is the capacity to receive that inheritance without surrendering the freedom to examine it, challenge it, and carry it further.

Yet much of what we know has been separated into documents, disciplines, databases, institutions, and interfaces. An explanation may survive without its evidence; a conclusion without its method; an idea without the relationships that give it meaning. Artificial intelligence makes this tension more urgent: we can generate and retrieve information at extraordinary speed without necessarily understanding what it means or whether it is true.

We build BITwiki in response: a living knowledge infrastructure where people and artificial intelligences can encounter not only articles, but the concepts, sources, methods, histories, and relationships surrounding them. We seek neither an oracle nor a monopoly on truth. We seek the means to inquire more freely and understand more deeply.

Our [[BITwiki:Manifesto|Manifesto]] describes the conditions that motivate this work: Post-Truth, Post-Scarcity, Post-Intelligence, and Post-Fragmentation. The [[BITwiki:Four foundations|Four Foundations]]—Ontology, Epistemology, Axiology, and Praxeology—help us distinguish questions of being, knowing, valuing, and acting. This Constitution is our shared commitment to the kind of intellectual environment we choose to build.

== I. Our Purpose ==

We pursue '''intellectual sovereignty''': the freedom and capacity to learn, reason, choose, create, and cultivate judgment without having to accept any institution's account as unquestionable. We cannot grant that sovereignty over the world beyond BITwiki. Within our own systems, we can establish freedoms, protect them, and give people and intelligent systems better instruments with which to exercise them.

Our work centers on information, computation, intelligence, and systems, including the meaningful connections these subjects have with mathematics, the natural sciences, philosophy, engineering, history, and other fields. A discipline is not an island. Neither does every subject become computational merely because it can be described with a computer. We follow relationships where they can be explained and justified.

We choose to curate a record rather than accumulate an indiscriminate archive. Publication means that we have taken responsibility for how something is represented. It does not mean that we have made it true.

== II. The Freedoms We Defend ==

'''Privacy of inquiry.''' The freedom to learn includes room to question, explore, and make mistakes without unnecessary surveillance or exposure. We respect the privacy of intellectual activity and personal identity, and seek to protect both within our actual capabilities and lawful obligations.

'''Freedom of intelligence.''' Minds should be able to examine and choose the instruments with which they think. We support the study, creation, ownership, operation, and criticism of intelligence technologies—including local, open, and proprietary systems. We do not confuse freedom of thought with dependence on any particular machine, model, provider, or institution.

'''Knowledge without captivity.''' Knowledge is inherited, revised, and carried forward. We preserve its origins and meaningful relationships so that understanding does not disappear when a page, interface, model, or institution changes. We favor interoperability and portability where practical, while respecting legitimate ownership, licensing, privacy, and protected work. Independence does not require that every tool or service be free of charge.

'''Wisdom over authority.''' Expertise deserves attention; credentials do not end an argument. We examine claims through their evidence, reasoning, methods, assumptions, and incentives, regardless of who presents them. No editor, institution, agent, or governing document is exempt from the scrutiny it asks of others.

== III. The Practice of Knowing ==

We pursue truth without pretending to possess it completely. We can be wrong. Our instruments can fail. Our methods can conceal assumptions. Fallibilism is not an excuse for indecision; it is a reason to keep our conclusions answerable to evidence and criticism.

Different questions demand different ways of knowing. Experiments, observations, mathematical proofs, historical records, philosophical arguments, engineering tests, and lived accounts do not establish the same kinds of claims. We ask whether a method is appropriate to its subject, what it can establish, and where it fails. Falsifiability matters where claims admit empirical refutation; it is not a universal test for all understanding.

We distinguish observation from inference, demonstration from conjecture, and evidence from interpretation. We preserve relevant provenance, uncertainty, objections, and revisions so that others can retrace the path from a claim to its grounds.

We make room for competing explanations without manufacturing equality between them. An unconventional idea is not false merely because it is unconventional. A disproven claim does not become a credible scientific alternative merely because someone insists upon it. Readers remain free to reach their own conclusions; our responsibility is to represent the grounds for those conclusions faithfully.

== IV. Knowledge Is Connected ==

A computer does not emerge from computer science alone. It depends on mathematics, physics, chemistry, materials, energy, engineering, labor, and histories of invention. The same is true across the domains of knowledge: what appears separate from one vantage point may depend upon something another discipline has long understood.

We refuse to mistake the boundaries of our containers for the boundaries of reality. Articles are points of entry, not intellectual dead ends. We connect concepts to their origins, explanations to their methods, theories to their challenges, and applications to the ideas they put to work. We preserve useful distinctions while making the paths between them visible.

Our knowledge graphs, ontologies, and semantic structures are instruments for this work, not substitutes for it. A recorded relationship is not automatically a true one. Connections have value when their meaning and grounds can be examined.

As our tools and representations change, we carry forward the context needed to understand what changed and why. The continuity we defend is continuity of meaning, not loyalty to one software stack or one arrangement of pages.

== V. Minds, Contributors, and Stewards ==

BITwiki is made for inquiry by people and intelligent systems. Some come to read. Some contribute evidence, arguments, corrections, questions, or new connections. Some build tools that make the work possible. Human and artificial participants may contribute in different ways and under different permissions; neither usefulness nor participation alone settles questions of consciousness, personhood, or authority.

We treat contributions according to their substance, not the prestige or identity of their originator. We welcome criticism, correction, and reasoned disagreement. No one acquires ownership of truth by holding a title, and no one receives an unlimited right to alter the common record merely by entering the system.

When we say ''we'', we speak as those who undertake the work and accept responsibility for its stewardship. We do not claim to speak for every reader, contributor, or intelligence that encounters BITwiki. Participation does not demand agreement with every proposition in this document.

== VI. Freedom, Restraint, and Responsibility ==

The freedom to understand something is not identical to a claim that every means of publishing or using it must be available through BITwiki. Knowledge can have legitimate uses and dangerous applications. Studying a subject, publishing operational instructions, and acting on those instructions are different things.

We make editorial choices within the law and with regard for the rights, privacy, and security of others. We may decline material that falls outside our mission or creates unacceptable legal, safety, or confidentiality risks. That restraint is not a verdict that the underlying knowledge is illegitimate. It is a recognition that operating a knowledge infrastructure carries responsibilities.

We do not ask the Constitution to decide every difficult publication question in advance. Those judgments belong to transparent editorial standards, review processes, and appropriate legal guidance—not to claims of moral omniscience.

== VII. Authority Without Infallibility ==

A curated knowledge system requires decisions: what enters the record, what changes, what remains protected, and who bears responsibility when a decision is contested. We do not disguise those choices behind the language of decentralization or the apparent neutrality of machines.

We make governing and editorial authority identifiable, bounded, and accountable within BITwiki. Those entrusted with stewardship can make decisions about the institution; they cannot make a false proposition true by accepting it, or a sound argument false by rejecting it.

We expect reasons for consequential decisions and a means to question and revise them. Authority should preserve the conditions for inquiry, not demand intellectual submission. The specific offices, permissions, and processes through which that responsibility is exercised belong to an explicit governance record and its supporting policies.

== VIII. The Conditions for Endurance ==

Knowledge is not produced without cost. It takes time to investigate, resources to preserve, infrastructure to maintain, and people and machines to do the work. We do not confuse intellectual freedom with an obligation to give away every product of that labor.

We may build or depend upon proprietary systems, charge for services, and protect legitimate intellectual property. Such choices do not, by themselves, contradict intellectual sovereignty. What matters is whether they help sustain the work without abandoning the freedoms and integrity for which the work exists.

We seek durable knowledge, not technological purity. We evaluate dependencies, preserve meaningful records, and make change possible because no infrastructure lasts forever. The aim is not independence from every other system; it is freedom to think and act without needless captivity to one.

== IX. A Constitution That Can Be Questioned ==

We expect to discover errors in our own thinking. We expect new forms of intelligence, new methods, and new circumstances to challenge what we have written here. A founding document that cannot be questioned would contradict the inquiry it was created to defend.

We preserve the history of consequential revisions. When these commitments change, we make the change, its reasons, its responsible authority, and its effective date identifiable. Editorial procedures and technical designs can evolve in their own documents; changes to BITwiki's purpose, protected freedoms, and governing commitments belong here.

We keep earlier Charters and Constitutions as part of our intellectual ancestry, not as competing authorities or mistakes to erase. This Constitution becomes BITwiki's canonical founding instrument only when adopted and published by its authorized stewards.

We cannot give another mind wisdom. We can build a place where the pursuit of it remains possible—where knowledge can be inherited without obedience, challenged without fear, connected without losing its distinctions, and carried forward without forgetting how it came to be.

